WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:12.864
Hey, Little Fox Readers.

2
00:00:13.644 --> 00:00:19.549
It's me, Jake, and I'm pretty excited to tell you about this week's landmark—the Sagrada Familia in Barcelona,

3
00:00:19.569 --> 00:00:25.955
Spain. That's because this huge Catholic cathedral is different from all the other landmarks we've studied so far:

4
00:00:26.535 --> 00:00:27.796
it's still being built!

5
00:00:28.737 --> 00:00:31.219
In fact, it won't be completed until around 2026.

6
00:00:33.004 --> 00:00:36.366
By then it will have taken over 140 years to build!

7
00:00:37.187 --> 00:00:43.811
The idea for the Sagrada Familia was born back in 1872 when a Spanish bookseller named Josep Maria

8
00:00:43.851 --> 00:00:49.355
Bocabella saw a beautiful church in Italy and dreamed of building a similar one in Barcelona.

9
00:00:50.195 --> 00:00:55.599
With the help of a society he'd founded earlier, Bocabella was able to begin construction on the Sagrada

10
00:00:55.639 --> 00:00:56.299
Familia by 1882.

11
00:00:58.896 --> 00:01:03.720
Over the years many different architects have overseen the construction of the Sagrada Familia.

12
00:01:04.641 --> 00:01:09.685
Most of these people have stayed close to the original design created by Antoni Gaudi, who was hired

13
00:01:09.725 --> 00:01:12.747
as the cathedral's main architect in 1883.

14
00:01:13.708 --> 00:01:19.973
In the beginning Gaudi worked on other buildings too, but from 1914 on, he devoted all his time

15
00:01:20.053 --> 00:01:21.254
to the Sagrada Familia.

16
00:01:22.095 --> 00:01:27.419
He became so devoted that he practically moved into his workshop inside the cathedral for the last few

17
00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:28.200
months of his life.

18
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:35.255
I guess all that hard work paid off because today the Sagrada Familia is known as Gaudi's masterpiece!

19
00:01:36.956 --> 00:01:43.939
On June 10, 1926, Gaudi died in a tragic accident: while walking to a church service, he was

20
00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:45.379
run over by a street tram.

21
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.021
He was buried in the Sagrada Familia's crypt, which had been finished by then.

22
00:01:50.922 --> 00:01:56.572
Fortunately Gaudi had known all along that the cathedral couldn't be completed during his lifetime, so he'd been

23
00:01:56.632 --> 00:02:00.054
thinking of ways to allow the project to continue even after his death.

24
00:02:00.955 --> 00:02:06.337
For example, he planned the construction in stages to make it easier for the generations of builders after

25
00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:12.100
him. He also spent a lot of time making models and drawing detailed plans of everything.

26
00:02:13.521 --> 00:02:18.464
The Sagrada Familia is a Catholic house of worship, but it can also be seen as a monument

27
00:02:18.484 --> 00:02:19.024
to nature.

28
00:02:19.976 --> 00:02:24.619
The main hall of the church, called the central nave, was designed to make visitors feel like they

29
00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:27.200
are standing under a glowing forest canopy.

30
00:02:28.121 --> 00:02:32.944
Huge, tree-like columns rise to a double roof, which reflects natural light.

31
00:02:33.904 --> 00:02:40.388
Also the gates, walls, and floors are covered with intricate patterns that look like honeycombs, palm leaves, and

32
00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:47.172
vines. Oh, and check out my favorite detail—spiral stairways that are modeled after snail shells!

33
00:02:48.832 --> 00:02:51.013
The outside of the building imitates nature too.

34
00:02:51.894 --> 00:02:56.596
The towers are made of shapes that resemble minerals and plants that can be found near Barcelona.

35
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:02.360
And if you look carefully at one of the building's sides, you can see detailed sculptures of almost

36
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.021
two hundred different species of plants and animals.

37
00:03:06.062 --> 00:03:10.564
Over the years people have had mixed reactions toward Gaudi's one-of-a-kind design.

38
00:03:11.485 --> 00:03:17.088
Some people have praised it as brilliant and imaginative while others have described it as bizarre or hideous.

39
00:03:18.048 --> 00:03:22.932
The famous British author George Orwell called it one of the ugliest buildings in the world!

40
00:03:23.792 --> 00:03:27.675
Personally, I think it looks pretty cool—like something from a fantasy movie.

41
00:03:28.436 --> 00:03:28.996
What do you think?

42
00:03:30.457 --> 00:03:37.282
The Sagrada Familia has been called a Bible in stone because the building's carvings, sculptures, and structures represent

43
00:03:37.322 --> 00:03:38.783
Catholic beliefs and stories.

44
00:03:39.744 --> 00:03:44.748
I think the best examples of these stories are the building's three sides, also called facades.

45
00:03:45.696 --> 00:03:49.417
The Nativity facade is the one with all the sculptures of plants and animals.

46
00:03:50.297 --> 00:03:54.079
It faces east, where the sun rises, to symbolize the birth of Jesus.

47
00:03:55.019 --> 00:04:02.041
The Passion facade was built with sad, dramatic sculptures and bone-shaped columns to symbolize the suffering and death

48
00:04:02.081 --> 00:04:02.621
of Jesus.

49
00:04:03.642 --> 00:04:07.343
It faces west, becoming shadowy and eerie as the sun sets.

50
00:04:08.243 --> 00:04:11.344
Gaudi actually hoped that this facade would scare people!

51
00:04:12.832 --> 00:04:19.278
While the Passion facade represents darkness and shadow, the Glory facade symbolizes eternal light and joy.

52
00:04:20.219 --> 00:04:24.763
It faces south, receiving lots of sunlight, to celebrate the resurrection of Jesus.

53
00:04:25.684 --> 00:04:29.988
Its construction began around 2000, and it will be the last facade to be completed.

54
00:04:30.929 --> 00:04:34.532
Once it's finished, it will serve as the main entrance to the Sagrada Familia.

55
00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:41.172
The cathedral will also have a total of 18 towers that symbolize important Christian figures such as the

56
00:04:41.232 --> 00:04:42.893
Virgin Mary and the apostles.

57
00:04:43.914 --> 00:04:50.058
The central tower, dedicated to Jesus, will be illuminated at night by the surrounding towers and will stretch

58
00:04:50.098 --> 00:04:56.742
an amazing 170 meters into the sky, making the Sagrada Familia the tallest cathedral in the world!

59
00:04:58.323 --> 00:05:02.566
Today over two million visitors come to Barcelona each year to see the cathedral.

60
00:05:03.387 --> 00:05:07.794
Tourists can take a trip down to the crypt or up to the towers, walk around the central

61
00:05:07.854 --> 00:05:12.877
nave, or explore some of the exhibitions on display, including a replica of Gaudi's workshop.

62
00:05:13.698 --> 00:05:18.581
Tourists also get to play an important role: the money they pay to visit the Sagrada Familia goes

63
00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:21.163
toward finishing it because it's an expiatory church.

64
00:05:22.164 --> 00:05:28.708
That means most of its funding comes from private donations and contributions—from wealthy people and organizations, as well

65
00:05:28.748 --> 00:05:30.409
as ordinary people like you and me!

66
00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:36.325
After the lesson about the Sagrada Familia, I thought it would be really neat if our class could

67
00:05:36.345 --> 00:05:37.326
send a donation too.

68
00:05:38.186 --> 00:05:41.708
To raise money, we sold cookies, brownies, and cupcakes after school.

69
00:05:42.609 --> 00:05:47.191
Mr. Fay told all the other classes to come and help support the Sagrada Familia, and our bake

70
00:05:47.231 --> 00:05:48.772
sale was a huge success!

71
00:05:49.793 --> 00:05:51.013
This week was awesome.

72
00:05:51.794 --> 00:05:56.376
Our class not only got to study a world landmark, we got to help build one too.

73
00:05:57.277 --> 00:06:02.340
And remember, the Sagrada Familia isn't finished yet, so it's not too late for you to help too!
